-- title:
--   Prime Hecke factorisation of a level-N connecting idèle
-- statement:
--   Let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ and let $\Lambda_1$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of the quaternion algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ which is a maximal order, in the sense that it contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and is finitely generated, and that every such submodule containing it coincides with it. Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let $m$ be a unit of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$, where $\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ is the finite adèle ring of $\mathbb{Q}$. Write $\widehat{\Lambda}$ for the additive subgroup of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ generated by the elements $z\otimes\alpha$ with $z\in\Lambda$ and $\alpha$ an adèle integral at every finite place. Assume $m\in\widehat{\Lambda_1}$, that $N\cdot m^{-1}\in\widehat{\Lambda_1}$, and that the intersection of $\Lambda_1$ with the submodule of elements of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ whose image under $z\mapsto z\otimes 1$ lies in $m\,\widehat{\Lambda_1}\,m^{-1}$ has relative index exactly $N$ in $\Lambda_1$, as additive subgroups. Then there exist a natural number $k$, a sequence of natural numbers $r_i$ and a sequence of units $s_i$ such that for every $i<k$ the number $r_i$ is prime and $s_i$ lies in the prime Hecke set of $\Lambda_1$ at $r_i$, that is $s_i\in\widehat{\Lambda_1}$, $r_i\cdot s_i^{-1}\in\widehat{\Lambda_1}$, $s_i^{-1}\notin\widehat{\Lambda_1}$ and $r_i^{-1}\cdot s_i\notin\widehat{\Lambda_1}$; moreover $\prod_{i<k}r_i=N$, and there is a unit $v$ stabilising the set $\widehat{\Lambda_1}$ under left multiplication with $m=s_0s_1\cdots s_{k-1}\,v$, the product being taken in the order given by $0,1,\dots,k-1$.
--
--   This is the invariant-factor (elementary divisor) analysis of the connecting idèle of an Eichler order of level $N$ inside a maximal order of a rational quaternion algebra, packaged as an ordered factorisation of that idèle into prime Hecke steps times a unit of the completed order. It is used in the Cherednik–Drinfeld part of the development, where the kernel of a map attached to $\Lambda_1\cap m\widehat{\Lambda_1}m^{-1}$ is shown to be cyclic by induction along such a prime factorisation.
